WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.790
I don't know where Donald Trump's impeachment
will ultimately end other than to say, as

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:29.960
I've said before, it's really unlikely that
impeachment is going to actually force Donald

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.620
Trump out of the White House through an impeachment
conviction.

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:38.860
I don't see that as even remotely likely,
but the possibility of an impeachment inquiry

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:44.890
and proceedings in the house, uh, is increasing
every day and it actually could be damaging

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:48.989
enough to Donald Trump to help remove him
in 2020 every single day.

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:54.620
Since the testimony of former special prosecutor
Robert Mueller last week, we have seen more

00:00:54.620 --> 00:01:00.470
and more Democrats say, I support an impeachment
inquiry and I support it now by the latest

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:07.870
count 105 Democrats in the House of Representatives
support starting an impeachment inquiry of

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:08.870
Donald Trump.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:13.460
Now as a reminder, an impeachment inquiry
is only step one.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:17.400
In order to remove a president via impeachment,
you need an impeachment inquiry, which leads

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.170
to formalizing the impeachment proceedings.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:26.289
Then you have an investigation and a trial
after which the Senate has to vote to convict

00:01:26.289 --> 00:01:27.750
with a two thirds majority.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.420
So we're very, very far from that.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:34.409
It is extraordinarily unlikely we are going
to get to that, but we may well get, and we

00:01:34.409 --> 00:01:39.500
could even argue that Jerry Nadler has already
defacto started an impeachment inquiry.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.970
Let's look at some examples of Democrats who
are now saying they're in favor of this.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:47.900
One example is congressman Denny heck who
said on Twitter quote, after considerable

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:51.679
reflection and prayerful consideration, don't
know why you need to pray to God to figure

00:01:51.679 --> 00:01:52.679
this one out.

00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:53.679
It's pretty clear.

00:01:53.679 --> 00:01:56.909
I have decided to support a formal impeachment
inquiry.

00:01:56.909 --> 00:02:01.890
Another example of congresswoman came Schrier
who said the people of the eighth district

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:05.969
of Washington elected me to protect their
health care and our environment.

00:02:05.969 --> 00:02:10.399
Those will always remain my focus while I
have the privilege of serving them in Congress.

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:14.819
They also helped me, uh, elected me to uphold
the constitution.

00:02:14.819 --> 00:02:18.210
So I am formally calling for unimpeachable
inquiry.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:24.590
This is now a defacto miniature get out the
vote campaign within Democrats in the House

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:30.000
of Representatives because the number right
now is 105 Democrats want an impeachment inquiry.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.450
Informally, they haven't signed on to anything,
but they have said, this is what we want.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:38.120
There are 435 seats in the House of Representatives.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:41.480
Half is two 17 plus one.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.719
Uh, there are 235 Democrats.

00:02:44.719 --> 00:02:50.260
Republicans are not needed in order to start
an impeachment inquiry that is important to

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:51.260
remember.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:55.689
Here is judiciary committee chairman Jerry
Nadler admitting that there are currently

00:02:55.689 --> 00:02:59.709
impeachment resolutions before the committee
that are being considered.

00:02:59.709 --> 00:03:02.680
Meaning this is very close to getting going.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:07.410
So, so how much of this debate over whether
or not the house isn't pursuing impeachment

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:08.970
is a semantic debate?

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:11.750
You have an impeachment inquiry going, don't
you?

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:14.349
We are examining, I'll just repeat what we
have said.

00:03:14.349 --> 00:03:15.739
In our court filings.

00:03:15.739 --> 00:03:18.439
We are, we have impeachment resolutions

00:03:18.439 --> 00:03:19.829
before the committee.

00:03:19.829 --> 00:03:27.401
We are conducting investigations to determine
whether we should report those, uh, impeachment

00:03:27.401 --> 00:03:30.720
or resolutions to the house or whether we
should draft our own and report them to the

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.819
house or we are considering those resolutions
and we will make a determination after we

00:03:34.819 --> 00:03:40.510
get more, uh, evidence, uh, as to the president's,
uh, crimes that we had from the Mallory report,

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:44.860
but also has to other things, uh, as to his
violations of the monuments closed.

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:51.439
Importantly, even Nancy Pelosi has sort of
changed her tune about impeachment, if not

00:03:51.439 --> 00:03:52.980
her substantive position.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:59.120
She now says that the house would decide whether
to begin impeachment proceedings quote, when

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:06.220
we have a best, strongest possible case suggesting
that maybe she's understanding that there

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:09.810
could be a strategic timing element to this
entire thing.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:14.840
Yet she is still saying that the time for
the impeachment inquiry is not right now,

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:19.400
which 105 other Democrats are saying is the
case, but she has started to shift her language

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:20.670
around as well.

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:27.100
So understand that any day, any day with relatively
little notice, we might learn that the house

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:28.730
has launched an impeachment inquiry.

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:34.060
And if you thought that Donald Trump's behavior
has been a Radek the last two and a half years,

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:36.080
you ain't seen nothing yet.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:39.770
Just wait until Donald Trump's behavior explodes.

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:41.240
If indeed this gets going.

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:42.660
Now it's important to understand.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:45.230
I've said it before and I'll say it again.

00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:46.510
Impeachment is not without risk.

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:51.730
There's the facts of the situation and there
are the political considerations, as I've

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:57.990
said before, the fact more than support and
impeachment inquiry in particular, Donald

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:03.190
Trump's endless obstructive acts to which
Robert Mueller testified last week.

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:06.440
Donald Trump's violations of the Amal humans
clause.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:10.450
I would even argue that Donald Trump's actions
in Yemen may be impeachable.

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:14.980
I did a segment about this about a month or
six weeks ago, and it is not receiving nearly

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:19.350
the attention as a sort of fodder for impeachment
that it probably should.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:26.050
Now given that there is not a path to an impeachment
conviction with the current Republican Senate,

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:32.290
there are ways in which a failed impeachment
attempt could hurt Democrats and embolden

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:33.290
Trump.

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:34.290
That's a real thing.

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:39.560
It's important to understand that the ideal
scenario is that the impeachment inquiry itself

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:44.190
is what exposes Donald Trump's wrongdoing
and it takes over the new cycle.

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:48.870
Trump is meanwhile trying to run for reelection
and yet media because of the impeachment inquiry,

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:50.450
is focused on what comes out of that.

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:57.220
That's the ideal scenario and in the end you
hope that the publicity around what Donald

00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:01.740
Trump has done in the content of the Mueller
report and the what would be just a treasure

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:08.070
trove of a, a horrible realities that would
be exposed by the impeachment inquiry would

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:14.770
get people not to vote for Trump in 2020 in
the end when you really think it through,

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:20.190
it's of should be an apolitical decision for
our elected officials to make.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:25.160
What I mean by that is I understand Democrats
thinking through the strategy of an impeachment

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:31.200
inquiry and the timing of an impeachment inquiry,
but congress is supposed to keep the executive

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:32.860
branch in check.

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:38.720
The best argument I've heard for why the impeachment
inquiry must start, and it must start now,

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.630
is simply that it's the job of Congress, period.

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:43.150
The political calculation.

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:45.030
I understand it's important.

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:50.940
I'm okay with Democrats timing the impeachment
inquiry to do maximum damage to Trump for

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:55.580
editorial purposes, but in the end, the best
argument for why this impeachment inquiries

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:59.900
should get going is that it is the duty of
Congress period.

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:04.710
And that all political considerations should
become secondary or even irrelevant to that

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:05.710
decision.

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:09.920
I know that it's not practical in this highly
politicized and partisan world in which we

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:12.760
live, but it is the job of Congress to do
this.

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:14.360
105 Democrats now say it's time to get it
going.

